OS-9/68000 FREEWARE COLLECTION==============================Over a thousand community programs for OS-9/68000, with their source anddocumentation where they survived, and where each one came from.OS-9 is Microware's, and still very much alive.  OS-9/68000 runsindustrial systems around the world, and OS-9 is still sold andsupported.This collection is the community's work.  Microware does not endorse itand is not part of it.  They allowed the runtime modules it needs -- cio,csl, csl020, math and math881 -- to ship on this disk; fpu ships under thegrant in DOC/fpu.doc.  SOURCES.txt records both.FINDING THINGS    man <name>        everything this disk has about a program    man -k <word>     the programs about <word>    DOC/CATEGORIES    every program, by what it is for    DOC/INDEX         every program, A to Z    DOC/START-HERE    fifteen that run with nothing set upSTARTING ITOn your own OS-9 system, give this disk a drive of its own, named both /ddand /h0.  Your system goes on /h1.  Then    bash /dd/SYS/loginsets PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAP, and commands go by name:    cookie    soundex </dd/SYS/motdWe have not done this on hardware ourselves.  DOC/README-RUNNING has whatis known.With os9exec, a community emulator for macOS, Linux and Windows:    OS9DISK=/full/path/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=/full/path/osk-freeware.dd \    OS9H1=<your OS-9>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginOS9H1 is optional; a few programs want your runb or shell from it.  Run theimage itself, not a copy unpacked into a directory: permissions and recordlocking work only on the image.KEEPING WHAT YOU LIKE    keep cookie       copies cookie, and the files it reads, to your /h1    kept              lists what you have kept    unkeep cookie     removes it, except files you have changedDOC/README-KEEP has more.WHERE TO LOOK    DOC/README-DOCS     every way to find out how to use something    DOC/README-RUNNING  arranging /dd, /h0 and /h1; DOS disks; GNU chess    DOC/STATUS          what runs, and what does not    DOC/ORIGINS         where each program came from    DOC/README-CIO      the starred programs    DOC/README-BUSERR   a bus error is usually the wrong terminal    DOC/README-MODULES  the files in CMDS that are not commands    DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT   math, math881 and fpu    SOURCES.txt         terms, build method, and what was left out and whyTERMSEach program keeps its author's terms; SOURCES.txt records them.  Eightcarry a condition their authors asked for -- no military use, or peacefulapplications only: disktest, help, hexed, hist, i_am_i, rxmod, txmod andspace.  DOC/EFFO-INFO has the words.  Please pass them on with thesoftware.